cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "entities-godot: the multiplayer-fabric fork of Godot Engine"
abstract: >
  Godot Engine, plus the multiplayer-fabric feature branches assembled on top of it.
  The engine is upstream's and so is its README; what this copy adds is the
  assembly -- d3d12 download retry, tapered capsule shapes, joint limitation, lasso,
  the HTTP/3 module, and the lint and CI work -- carried on the multiplayer-fabric
  and multiplayer-fabric-base branches. This file exists because a fork is the
  repository whose provenance most needs stating: it is upstream's code plus a branch
  nobody upstream has, and nothing else in the tree says which is which.
authors:
  - name: "Godot Engine contributors"
  - family-names: Linietsky
    given-names: Juan
  - family-names: Manzur
    given-names: Ariel
version: 0.1.0-dev.1
date-released: "2026-08-17"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric/entities-godot"
keywords:
  - godot
  - game-engine
  - fork
references:

  - type: software
    title: "Godot Engine"
    authors:
      - name: "Godot Engine contributors"
      - family-names: Linietsky
        given-names: Juan
      - family-names: Manzur
        given-names: Ariel
    url: "https://github.com/godotengine/godot"
    notes: >
      Upstream. This repository carries no fork flag on GitHub, because it was made by
      pushing an existing history rather than by pressing the button, so the evidence
      is stated instead of fetched: the README opens "# Godot Engine" and links
      godotengine.org nineteen times. It tracks the gyre branch while upstream's own
      default is master, which is one of two revisions in the manifest that a reader
      would not guess.

  - type: software
    title: "entities-assembly: the branch assembly that produces this fork"
    authors:
      - family-names: Lee
        given-names: "K. S. Ernest (iFire)"
    url: "https://github.com/v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric/entities-assembly"
    notes: >
      The multiplayer-fabric branches here are force-pushed by that repository from its
      gitassembly file. This history is a product of that recipe, so the list of what
      the fork adds is kept there rather than restated here where it would drift.
